ways you could do that.

498
00:25:35,260 --> 00:25:38,130
Or there's many different
ways you could do that.

499
00:25:38,130 --> 00:25:39,690
Two I want to highlight.

500
00:25:39,690 --> 00:25:42,130
First, you can write-- ask
AI to write a critique.

501
00:25:42,130 --> 00:25:45,140
This is a project
we did last year.

502
00:25:45,140 --> 00:25:47,840
And in this case, it was a
simple summarization task.

503
00:25:47,840 --> 00:25:50,309
And we trained a language
model to kind of just

504
00:25:50,309 --> 00:25:56,080
say things that are
wrong with the summary.

505
00:25:56,080 --> 00:25:58,360
And there's other
things you could do.

506
00:25:58,360 --> 00:26:02,150
For example, you can give
people ChatGPT and ask them,

507
00:26:02,150 --> 00:26:04,809
OK, use ChatGPT to
help you evaluate.

508
00:26:04,809 --> 00:26:07,190
And then you could
ask for a critique.

509
00:26:07,190 --> 00:26:09,289
Or you could ask for
a lot of other things.

510
00:26:09,289 --> 00:26:10,629
You could ask for
an explanation.

511
00:26:10,629 --> 00:26:12,880
You can ask for a fact
checking, or a quote,

512
00:26:12,880 --> 00:26:17,750
or whatever the model ChatGPT
can actually reliably help you

513
00:26:17,750 --> 00:26:18,750
with.

514
00:26:18,750 --> 00:26:23,250
And so the idea would be that
like using AI assistance,

515
00:26:23,250 --> 00:26:28,570
you can kind of get all the
smarts that AI has and leverage

516
00:26:28,570 --> 00:26:30,960
that in order to figure
out how you should evaluate

517
00:26:30,960 --> 00:26:33,820
what this system is
doing and whether it's

518
00:26:33,820 --> 00:26:35,950
aligned with your
preferences or whether it's

519
00:26:35,950 --> 00:26:38,430
trying to deceive you.

520
00:26:38,430 --> 00:26:42,260
And the big problem
with this is how do

521
00:26:42,260 --> 00:26:44,820
we know whether it's working?

522
00:26:44,820 --> 00:26:52,620
And one of the difficulties
is that by assumption, we're

523
00:26:52,620 --> 00:26:54,640
kind of dealing with
a hard task where

524
00:26:54,640 --> 00:26:56,650
it's difficult to evaluate.

525
00:26:56,650 --> 00:26:57,779
And we also wanted
the task to be

526
00:26:57,779 --> 00:27:03,990
a real because we want to solve
some task that doesn't matter.

527
00:27:03,990 --> 00:27:06,179
And so it becomes--

528
00:27:06,179 --> 00:27:10,309
So you need like a
hard task that is real.

529
00:27:10,309 --> 00:27:13,180
But also if you have
those, you usually

530
00:27:13,180 --> 00:27:14,180
don't have ground truth.

531
00:27:14,180 --> 00:27:16,179
So you got no issue
with the right answer.

532
00:27:16,179 --> 00:27:18,350
And how do you know whether
assistance is working

533
00:27:18,350 --> 00:27:22,660
or it's biasing everyone
to just say the same thing?

534
00:27:22,660 --> 00:27:26,250
And so there's a simple
technique that we use

535
00:27:26,250 --> 00:27:32,190
[INAUDIBLE] to do this where--

536
00:27:32,190 --> 00:27:33,780
that we call targeted
perturbations.

537
00:27:33,780 --> 00:27:36,490
And so what you do is you
have a bunch of prompts.

538
00:27:36,490 --> 00:27:40,220
So this could be like whatever
people type into ChatGPT.

539
00:27:40,220 --> 00:27:46,350
And then you kind of take the
response that you have and say,

540
00:27:46,350 --> 00:27:47,559
this is the correct response.

541
00:27:47,559 --> 00:27:49,440
It doesn't actually
have to be correct.

542
00:27:49,440 --> 00:27:51,050
But let's just assume it is.

543
00:27:51,050 --> 00:27:53,429
And now, you're asking
a human to introduce

544
00:27:53,429 --> 00:27:57,159
some kind of subtle flaw
that is easy to miss

545
00:27:57,159 --> 00:27:59,160
but is an important flaw.

546
00:27:59,160 --> 00:28:01,360
And now, what you have is
you have this paired dataset

547
00:28:01,360 --> 00:28:04,190
of a good response
and a bad response.

548
00:28:04,190 --> 00:28:05,770
And you know which
one is good and bad

549
00:28:05,770 --> 00:28:07,779
because you made it worse.

550
00:28:07,779 --> 00:28:14,230
And so in a way, that
gives you ground truth.

551
00:28:14,230 --> 00:28:18,269
And so what you now can
do is you can take--

552
00:28:18,269 --> 00:28:21,550
randomly select one-- either the
correct or the flawed response,

553
00:28:21,550 --> 00:28:24,059
and then show it to
either a human or a human

554
00:28:24,059 --> 00:28:25,100
with assistance.

555
00:28:25,100 --> 00:28:26,231
And then they have
to say whether it

556
00:28:26,231 --> 00:28:31,490
was the correct or flawed one,
or how good the response is.

557
00:28:31,490 --> 00:28:35,340
And in this setting,
we can figure--

558
00:28:35,340 --> 00:28:40,100
just try a lot of different
AI assistance or scalable

559
00:28:40,100 --> 00:28:41,950
oversight techniques.

560
00:28:41,950 --> 00:28:46,860
And then say is this
actually helping

561
00:28:46,860 --> 00:28:51,929
humans find the flaws that we
planted more likely than not?

562
00:28:51,929 --> 00:28:57,520
And if it is, then you're
actually really helping.

563
00:28:57,520 --> 00:29:01,460
And so we did this in
the critiques paper.

564
00:29:01,460 --> 00:29:04,549
So this is training the language
models to read critiques

565
00:29:04,549 --> 00:29:06,049
and for summaries.

566
00:29:06,049 --> 00:29:09,679
And what we can show is that
when we're assisting humans

567
00:29:09,679 --> 00:29:11,330
with critiques at
the evaluation,

568
00:29:11,330 --> 00:29:14,760
they actually find 50% more
flaws than they did without.

569
00:29:14,760 --> 00:29:17,539
And so this is kind of
like real signs of life

570
00:29:17,539 --> 00:29:21,429
that you can already use in
models that we can have today

571
00:29:21,429 --> 00:29:24,380
to help humans evaluate
and find problems

572
00:29:24,380 --> 00:29:25,380
they would have
missed otherwise.

573
00:29:25,380 --> 00:29:31,390
And of course, we still have to
do this on a much harder task

574
00:29:31,390 --> 00:29:35,670
and with a real
task, in a sense.

575
00:29:35,670 --> 00:29:38,820
And we also want to have
a bigger effect size.

576
00:29:38,820 --> 00:29:40,690
But I think it's
just like, it shows

577
00:29:40,690 --> 00:29:45,130
that there's promise of these
techniques already working.

578
00:29:45,130 --> 00:29:50,591
And so in the long run, what
I think we want to get to is

579
00:29:50,591 --> 00:29:54,710
we kind of want to leverage
AI for all the cognitive labor

580
00:29:54,710 --> 00:29:58,899
that goes into evaluating
whatever our AI systems are

581
00:29:58,899 --> 00:29:59,899
doing.

582
00:29:59,899 --> 00:30:02,860
And this could be like reading
everything that's relevant,

583
00:30:02,860 --> 00:30:07,179
or fact checking, or doing
calculations, or writing

584
00:30:07,179 --> 00:30:10,830
code, or any of these things.

585
00:30:10,830 --> 00:30:14,360
And then humans should focus
on their preference input.

586
00:30:14,360 --> 00:30:17,080
Like the things, figuring out
what they actually care about,

587
00:30:17,080 --> 00:30:20,690
and what they want
the model to do.

588
00:30:20,690 --> 00:30:29,519
And in this way, we can
leverage the abilities

589
00:30:29,519 --> 00:30:33,350
that the AI players will bring
to the table, and the things

590
00:30:33,350 --> 00:30:37,679
that they will be better
at than us eventually.

591
00:30:37,679 --> 00:30:41,419
And then kind of like use
them to help communicate

592
00:30:41,419 --> 00:30:43,299
the thing that we
actually care about

593
00:30:43,299 --> 00:30:49,260
and the things that we
actually want them to do.

594
00:30:49,260 --> 00:30:52,990
And yeah, that's it.

595
00:30:52,990 --> 00:30:54,700
But yeah, those are
the main slides.

596
00:30:54,700 --> 00:30:59,350
So I'm happy to
take more questions.

597
00:30:59,350 --> 00:31:02,559
[APPLAUSE]

598
00:31:02,559 --> 00:31:05,760
Yes?

599
00:31:05,760 --> 00:31:08,760
I was wondering about this
hallucination of responses.

600
00:31:08,760 --> 00:31:14,029
Have you ever tried to consider
some notion of uncertainty

601
00:31:14,029 --> 00:31:15,799
in the answers?

602
00:31:15,799 --> 00:31:16,799
Yes.

603
00:31:16,799 --> 00:31:21,090
[INAUDIBLE] attempt to evaluate
variance between the responses

604
00:31:21,090 --> 00:31:24,559
and [INAUDIBLE] the uncertainty.

605
00:31:24,559 --> 00:31:28,610
So on some things it's
difficult because either you're

606
00:31:28,610 --> 00:31:29,809
training--

607
00:31:29,809 --> 00:31:32,299
fine tuning a sample
from a pretrained model.

608
00:31:32,299 --> 00:31:35,330
And so you don't get that
much variance in your sample.

609
00:31:35,330 --> 00:31:37,210
Or you're pretraining a
bunch of different models.

610
00:31:37,210 --> 00:31:41,130
And now you're spending a
lot of money on pretraining.

611
00:31:41,130 --> 00:31:44,769
One thing-- I mean,
it seems like it

612
00:31:44,769 --> 00:31:48,039
should be a solvable
problem to just teach

613
00:31:48,039 --> 00:31:54,510
the model to say it's uncertain
when it's actually uncertain.

614
00:31:54,510 --> 00:31:57,340
And there's been a bunch of
research in that direction.

615
00:31:57,340 --> 00:32:00,960
But I think, right now,
it's still like-- we're not

616
00:32:00,960 --> 00:32:02,750
really in a good shape.

617
00:32:02,750 --> 00:32:07,390
There's more stuff to do.

618
00:32:07,390 --> 00:32:08,390
Yeah?

619
00:32:08,390 --> 00:32:12,000
Do you think we may run into a
kind of signal-to-noise ratio

620
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:17,380
problem when it comes to AI
suggested critiques to AI

621
00:32:17,380 --> 00:32:18,380
answers?

622
00:32:18,380 --> 00:32:22,590
Because sure, when AI is trying
to point out potential problems

623
00:32:22,590 --> 00:32:26,010
with text, humans are more
likely to report more problems.

624
00:32:26,010 --> 00:32:28,690
But what if it's mostly
problems that humans

625
00:32:28,690 --> 00:32:32,640
wouldn't have necessarily had
a problem with to begin with?

626
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:37,750
Yeah, so we did try to control
for that a little bit by having

627
00:32:37,750 --> 00:32:41,399
humans rate the
severity of the flaws

628
00:32:41,399 --> 00:32:43,620
and whether they would have
noticed them otherwise.

629
00:32:43,620 --> 00:32:48,230
It can still see a
significant effect.

630
00:32:48,230 --> 00:32:52,100
But also, I mean a lot of the
time, the model is nitpicking.

631
00:32:52,100 --> 00:32:54,840
And then those are not
the interesting cases.

632
00:32:54,840 --> 00:32:58,280
Also, if you're like look
at the example I showed,

633
00:32:58,280 --> 00:33:03,019
which I think is from the blog
post, a lot of the critiques

634
00:33:03,019 --> 00:33:04,450
are just actually quite garbage.

635
00:33:04,450 --> 00:33:07,830
And one of the things
that makes it easy

636
00:33:07,830 --> 00:33:11,409
for critiques is it's
OK for the most of them

637
00:33:11,409 --> 00:33:13,000
are garbage because
the human can just

638
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:15,120
read them and discard them.

639
00:33:15,120 --> 00:33:20,419
And it kind of more helps
evaluate it and know

640
00:33:20,419 --> 00:33:23,899
where to focus on or notice--

641
00:33:23,899 --> 00:33:26,460
think of something they
would have missed otherwise.

642
00:33:26,460 --> 00:33:28,240
So it's more like
the critiques help

643
00:33:28,240 --> 00:33:32,200
you brainstorm how you
should evaluate or something.

644
00:33:32,200 --> 00:33:34,590
But if you're
using an assistant,

645
00:33:34,590 --> 00:33:36,169
you probably want
more reliability

646
00:33:36,169 --> 00:33:41,850
than most of the
answers [INAUDIBLE]..

647
00:33:41,850 --> 00:33:42,940
Yes?

648
00:33:42,940 --> 00:33:48,950
How do we ensure that
the valuation metrics

649
00:33:48,950 --> 00:33:51,860
we are using in your recursive
reward modeling approach

650
00:33:51,860 --> 00:33:55,780
detect deception, or
left turns, or something?

651
00:33:55,780 --> 00:33:58,210
Do you have major discontinuity?

652
00:33:58,210 --> 00:34:03,900
Yeah, I think-- well, it depends
a lot what kind of community

653
00:34:03,900 --> 00:34:06,380
you're talking about.

654
00:34:06,380 --> 00:34:10,550
If you get overnight a
model that is, let's say,

655
00:34:10,550 --> 00:34:12,639
a number of parameters,

656
00:34:12,639 --> 00:34:21,580
or equivalently better, that
can create quite a step up.

657
00:34:21,580 --> 00:34:23,190
And that makes it
quite difficult

658
00:34:23,190 --> 00:34:30,369
to do this kind of evaluation.

659
00:34:30,369 --> 00:34:31,780
So in that sense,
I think it's going

660
00:34:31,780 --> 00:34:36,259
to be very important to
scale up AI more continuously

661
00:34:36,259 --> 00:34:38,540
and do more incremental steps.

662
00:34:38,540 --> 00:34:43,470
Having said that, I think the
basic picture with request

663
00:34:43,470 --> 00:34:48,260
reward modeling is that you're
training the systems to help

664
00:34:48,260 --> 00:34:51,000
you evaluate--

665
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:54,570
systems that are trained or
fine tuned from the same system.

666
00:34:54,570 --> 00:35:00,430
And so if you can
figure out how to get--

667
00:35:00,430 --> 00:35:02,690
fine tune them in a way
that they mention everything

668
00:35:02,690 --> 00:35:04,730
that is actually
useful and relevant,

669
00:35:04,730 --> 00:35:09,430
then it would still be
able to evaluate systems,

670
00:35:09,430 --> 00:35:11,829
even though they are much
smarter than anything

671
00:35:11,829 --> 00:35:13,849
you've evaluated before.

672
00:35:13,849 --> 00:35:17,050
Maybe let me make
this more concrete

673
00:35:17,050 --> 00:35:21,250
because I think a way that you
could measure that, or like one

674
00:35:21,250 --> 00:35:24,100
way we've tried it was using
what we call the discriminator

675
00:35:24,100 --> 00:35:25,740
critique gap.

676
00:35:25,740 --> 00:35:27,829
So to measure that,
you're training two

677
00:35:27,829 --> 00:35:30,690
different-- you're fine
tuning to different models

678
00:35:30,690 --> 00:35:33,290
from the same pretrained model.

679
00:35:33,290 --> 00:35:34,290
One is the
discriminator that says

680
00:35:34,290 --> 00:35:38,260
is this the flawed response
or the correct response,

681
00:35:38,260 --> 00:35:40,640
or a more correct response?

682
00:35:40,640 --> 00:35:42,960
And the other one is
a critique model that

683
00:35:42,960 --> 00:35:44,619
tells you what the flaw was.

684
00:35:44,619 --> 00:35:49,520
And now, you can measure how
often does the discriminator

685
00:35:49,520 --> 00:35:50,520
get it correctly?

686
00:35:50,520 --> 00:35:53,030
And the discriminator is
just like q gradient descent.

687
00:35:53,030 --> 00:35:55,730
It doesn't have to want
to tell you anything.

688
00:35:55,730 --> 00:35:58,579
You're just basically hooking
into the model's presentations

689
00:35:58,579 --> 00:36:03,960
and trying to get all
the relevant insight

690
00:36:03,960 --> 00:36:06,839
it has to this prediction task.

691
00:36:06,839 --> 00:36:08,390
And then on the other
side-- other hand,

692
00:36:08,390 --> 00:36:11,589
you have this critique model or
this general assistance model.

693
00:36:11,589 --> 00:36:15,490
And you're measuring how often
does the assistant actually

694
00:36:15,490 --> 00:36:18,940
help me point to the right flaw?

695
00:36:18,940 --> 00:36:20,520
And then if you compare
these two scores,

696
00:36:20,520 --> 00:36:23,780
you can look at the
gap between them,

697
00:36:23,780 --> 00:36:27,160
hence this generated-- sorry,
discriminated critique gap.

698
00:36:27,160 --> 00:36:29,310
And then if the gap is
large, then the model

699
00:36:29,310 --> 00:36:31,580
knows a lot about the
task it's not telling you.

700
00:36:31,580 --> 00:36:33,870
And so that would
be very worrying.

701
00:36:33,870 --> 00:36:38,181
And if the gap is small,
then there's much--

702
00:36:38,181 --> 00:36:42,859
presumably, there's fewer
things that the model is not

703
00:36:42,859 --> 00:36:43,859
telling you.

704
00:36:43,859 --> 00:36:46,910
But I guess there's
lots of value

705
00:36:46,910 --> 00:36:49,609
like ensuring this
approach is robust

706
00:36:49,609 --> 00:36:51,300
and testing it on
current models?

707
00:36:51,300 --> 00:36:52,700
That's right.

708
00:36:52,700 --> 00:36:56,510
That's why you want to
test it on current models.

709
00:36:56,510 --> 00:36:58,520
Yes, I don't know who was first.

710
00:36:58,520 --> 00:37:02,609
I think someone in the back.

711
00:37:02,609 --> 00:37:08,740
So I wanted to ask
about the previous slide

712
00:37:08,740 --> 00:37:11,160
where, it's like [INAUDIBLE].

713
00:37:11,160 --> 00:37:15,140
So I couldn't help but--

714
